<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Welcome to the PRADO Quickstart Tutorial</h1>
<div id="intro" class="block-content">
<p>
This Quickstart tutorial is provided to help you quickly start building your own Web applications based on PRADO version 4.x.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
If you are already a PRADO user and would like to learn what enhancements are available for each new version, please check out the <a href="?page=GettingStarted.NewFeatures">new features</a> page. Otherwise, the following sections are helpful for newbies.
</p>

<p>
You may refer to the following resources if you find this tutorial does not fulfill all your needs.
</p>
</div>
<ul class="block-content" id="intro-links">
  <li><a href="https://pradosoft.github.io/docs/manual/">PRADO API Documentation</a>, the reference for every class and property.</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/pradosoft/prado">The PRADO repository</a>, including <tt>HISTORY.md</tt> and <tt>UPGRADE.md</tt>.</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/pradosoft/prado/issues">PRADO Issue Tracker</a>.</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/pradosoft/prado-demos">The demo applications</a>, which include the source of this tutorial.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How this tutorial is organized</h2>

<ul class="block-content">
  <li><b>Getting started</b> covers installing PRADO, a first application, and the command line tool.</li>
  <li><b>Fundamentals</b> explains the parts a PRADO application is built from: components, controls, pages, modules, services and behaviors.</li>
  <li><b>Configurations</b> covers the application and page configuration files, and the template syntax.</li>
  <li><b>Control reference</b> documents the controls, both the ordinary ones and the AJAX-enabled active controls, each with a runnable sample.</li>
  <li><b>Working with databases</b> covers the data access layer, Active Record and the SqlMap data mapper.</li>
  <li><b>Advanced topics</b> covers themes, authentication, caching, logging, internationalization, scheduled tasks and more.</li>
</ul>

<p class="block-content">
Most pages in the control reference end with a <b>Run Sample</b> and <b>View Source</b>
pair that opens a working example and its code. Where a page or a section begins with a
line reading <i>Available from Prado versions</i>, that line gives the release the feature
first shipped in.
</p>
</com:TContent>
